\section*{Remarks}

\paragraph{Snapshot semantics.}
Line~3 of both algorithms caches $\bm{U}_{kf(t-1)}$ before the sweep loop, and
the recursion
\begin{equation}
  \bm{U}_{kft} = \alpha \bm{U}_{kf(t-1)}
                 + (1-\alpha)\, \varphi(r_{kt})\, \xft \xft\herm
  \label{eq:cov}
\end{equation}
is rebuilt from that snapshot on every sweep. Only $r_{kt}$ is refreshed
between sweeps, which is the purpose of the inner loop; the decay is applied
once per frame.

\paragraph{Snapshot of row $k$ in Algorithm~\ref{alg:iss}.}
Line~11 caches $\wkf\herm$ before the $m$-loop so that all $K$ rank-one updates
within one $k$-step use the pre-update row, matching the simultaneous form
$\Wf \gets \Wf - \vkf \wkf\herm$. Since $v_{mkf}$ reads row $m$, which the loop
has not yet written, the sequential in-place updates are equivalent to the
simultaneous one.

\paragraph{Why $\mathcal{I}_{t}$ works for ISS but not IP.}
The ISS update is equivalent to updating the steering vector $\bm{a}_{kf}$, a
\emph{column} of the mixing matrix:
\begin{equation}
  \bm{a}_{kf} + \bm{u}_{kf}
  = \frac{1}{1 - v_{kkf}}
    \Bigl( \bm{a}_{kf} + \sum_{m \neq k} v_{mkf} \bm{a}_{mf} \Bigr).
  \label{eq:steering}
\end{equation}
When one source moves, exactly one column of $\bm{A}_{ft}$ goes stale, so
restricting $\mathcal{I}_{t}$ to that index repairs precisely what changed. IP
updates a \emph{row} of $\Wf$, and no row-wise operation corrects a single
column of $\bm{A}_{ft}$; a restricted IP update therefore reduces cost but
cannot track. The covariance loop stays over all $k$ in both algorithms,
since $\bm{U}_{mf}$ for $m \neq k$ appears in the denominators of
\eqref{eq:steering}'s coefficients.

\paragraph{Cost.}
Per frame, the covariance update is $O(N_{\mathrm{iter}} K^{2} F)$ per source
and is identical in both algorithms. The demixing update is
$O(|\mathcal{I}_{t}| K^{2} F)$ for ISS against $O(|\mathcal{I}_{t}| K^{3} F)$
for IP, so any complexity advantage lives entirely in the second phase and is
best measured there rather than in end-to-end runtime.

